Canvas Skies 0.2.0 RC6
======================

An original First World War flying game for AmigaOS 3. Fly with pilot
Neddy Seagoon of the Royal Flying Corps through eight chapters, from the
first observation flights of August 1914 to the eleventh hour of the
Armistice.

New in RC6
----------
- The Christmas truce of 1914 flown in full: out over men of both
  armies meeting round braziers in no man's land, the pudding dropped
  on their aerodrome, and home to land, with nobody firing
- The 1914 cockpit patrols give Tom an observer's work: mark a column,
  drop a message bag on headquarters (B), photograph a battery, and
  signal the fall of our guns' shells (S)
- Tom's Lewis gun depresses to thirty degrees below level over the
  sides, and some attackers come up from below
- Squalls in the cockpit and the rear seat too, and a rain haze when
  you fly into one
- The officers in the mess painted as men by lamplight, and each
  evening's story told by what they are doing
- Wingmen carry their wounds from one sortie to the next, and a
  grounding is kept if you quit
- We Remember: a roll call over the old front at the end of the game
- Every chapter has four side-on, four top-down, two dogfight and two
  rear-seat sorties, and every order can be met by a careful pilot
- The aerodrome films mended: the 1914 machine climbs away, the flight
  of three stays in the picture, and the muddy field is mud

Changes in RC5
--------------
- Missions with something to do all the way: Archie bursting where you
  would have been, ground machine guns, riflemen taking pot shots,
  kite balloons on their cables, squalls, scouts head-on and on your
  tail going home; 1914's work of marking columns, dropping message
  bags on headquarters and photographing batteries; and a run home to
  land at the aerodrome instead of flying on with nothing to do
- Take-offs and landings on old film before and after each sortie, with
  their own sound; Enter or fire cuts them short
- The 1914 observer's carbine swings right round the tail, up and down,
  and the rear-seat attackers come from the flanks and break off when
  hit
- The mess in three evenings: a quiet one, a rowdy one after a victory,
  and a wake after a loss, with the squadron round the table by
  lamplight
- The Officer's Preferences on the officers' menu: keep the art in
  memory, music and effects volume, full screen
- Each scene loads only its own art: the game now runs in about 25 MB
  of fast memory instead of 63
- Leaving full screen or quitting from it now gives Workbench back
  intact

Changes in RC4
--------------
- Every picture redrawn as finished art: the cockpit's wings, struts
  and wires; the medals and the office crest; the squadron mess and the
  picture house, lit by their lamps, stoves and the screen, with a hall
  full of men watching the newsreel
- Real trenches seen from the air and from the ground: fire bays and
  traverses, sandbags, duckboards, water in the sumps and wire in front
- The papers: the memorandum and the court martial typed on foolscap
  with a rubber stamp, the new-machines cards, and the newspaper tied
  with twine on its bundle
- Painted machines for every year, top-down targets that stand off the
  ground, soft smoke, sharper explosions, and the village and the
  refugee cart rebuilt
- The empty chair in the mess the night a pilot is lost

New in 0.2.0
------------
- Cinematic cockpit view: painted sky, textured ground, shaded aircraft
  with struts and markings, a living cockpit with working dials
- Open-sky dogfights with thinking enemy pilots and your own flight
- A pilot's career: victories, promotion and medals
- The story told as a war diary, written on the notebook's own lines
- The morning paper, quoting real cable news of 1914-1918, a
  late-edition clipping after each debrief, and the Book of Press
  Cuttings on the officers' menu
- The Christmas truce of 1914, a night patrol against an airship over
  Essex, and the Gotha bombers over the Thames
- Dawn at the hangars before every sortie, and the mess that evening
  after a mission won
- Period gramophone records for each year of the war
- 1914 is flown with a rifle and a revolver, as it was

Changes since RC2
-----------------
- The cockpit seen from the pilot's seat: the top wing overhead, struts
  close beside the gun, wires in the bays; the guns follow the year
- The planes change as the war goes on, ours and theirs
- Top-down raids on convoys, railways, stations, aerodromes and trench
  lines; spare the ambulances, the villages and our own line, or answer
  for it, up to a court martial
- A side-on bomb that bursts too close hurts your own machine
- The morning paper set like a real one, on its tied bundle
- The picture house behind the lines, showing real film of the war
- Period typefaces, a diary on level lines, tracer streaks that follow
  their flight

Changes since RC1
-----------------
- Only armed sorties carry guns; the 1914 cockpit has no guns
- The cockpit sky no longer shows a flat slab in a bank
- Every sortie ends cleanly, with the wreck shown before the hospital,
  and is saved honestly; losing the last life costs a chapter, not the
  whole career
- Every mission now asks something of you; the Armistice patrols are
  quiet, the raiders can get through, and the truce needs its pudding
- Many story details now follow what happened that day
- Faster drawing on a 68040

Requirements
------------
- 68040 with FPU
- RTG graphics: the game opens a 640x400 window on a 16-bit (or deeper)
  Workbench screen
- GFOpenUp's GFOpenGPU part, with its SDL 2 runtime
  (LIBS:GFOpenGPU/SDL2.module and LIBS:gfopengpu.library); tested with
  GFOpenUp 0.8.26. Without it the game stops at once and says so
- AHI for sound
- 32 MB of fast memory; with 96 MB or more free the art is kept in
  memory once loaded (the Officer's Preferences can change this)

Installing
----------
Drag the CanvasSkies drawer anywhere, for example to Work:Games. Start
the game with the "Start Canvas Skies" icon, or from a Shell:

  CD Work:Games/CanvasSkies
  Stack 262144
  CanvasSkies

Keep the "assets" drawer beside the program. The film, fonts and music
are credited in CANVAS_SKIES_SOURCES.md, assets/canvas-skies/MUSIC_LICENSE.md
and the Fonts-Licences drawer.

Controls
--------
Menus            Up/Down select, Enter accepts, Escape returns
Press cuttings   Left/Right turn the page, Enter returns
Side/top-down    Arrow keys move, X/Z speed, Space fires, B bombs
Cockpit          Left/Right bank, Down pulls up, Up pushes down,
                 X/Z throttle, Space fires, H turns for home
Rear gunner      Arrow keys traverse and elevate, Space fires
1914 tasks       B drops the message bag; fly steady over the battery
                 to photograph it; in the cockpit, S signals the fall
                 of our guns' shells
Films            Enter, Space or Escape skips
Display          F9 fullscreen, F7 half detail, F8 full flight model
                 (and the Officer's Preferences on the officers' menu)
A joystick in port 2 flies the aircraft and fires.

